Result / Learning Outcome:

LDA is a supervised technique used for both dimensionality reduction and classification.
It finds new axes that maximize the distance between class means (between-class scatter, Sb) and minimize the spread within each class (within-class scatter, Sw).
The directions are the eigenvectors of inv(Sw) · Sb. The eigenvalue shows how much class separation each direction captures.
For C classes, LDA gives at most C − 1 useful discriminants. Here there are 3 classes, so there are 2, and the first one (LD1) alone captures about 98.8% of the separation.
The Iris data was reduced from 4 features to 2 and still classified with 97.78% accuracy.
